# RAG Baseline Metrics Audit & Correction Report

> **Corpus**: *The Comedy of Errors document.pdf* (~203 indexed semantic chunks)  
> **Evaluation Mode**: Metric Audit & Baseline Verification (Zero retrieval/architecture changes)  
> **Retrieval Architecture**: Semantic Search (HNSW / Qdrant) + BM25 Search + Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF, 50-50 weight)

---

## Executive Summary

This report performs a comprehensive audit of the baseline evaluation. Every metric was audited directly against raw retrieval logs and verified gold annotations. Calculation bugs (such as duplicate parent section exclusions affecting gold hits and confusion between Primary vs. Evidence Precision) have been corrected.

### Core Audit Discoveries

1. **RRF Degradation Confirmed Empirically**:
   Equal-weighted RRF (50-50) consistently **degrades** pure Semantic Search across every metric:
   * **Recall@10**: Semantic **54.2%** $\rightarrow$ RRF **50.0%**
   * **MRR**: Semantic **0.400** $\rightarrow$ RRF **0.263**
   * **nDCG@10**: Semantic **0.515** $\rightarrow$ RRF **0.401**
   * **Evidence Completeness**: Semantic **50.0%** $\rightarrow$ RRF **33.3%**

2. **BM25 Failure on Play Corpus**:
   BM25 Search achieved only **16.7% Hit Rate** and **4.2% Recall@10** (retrieving only 1 gold chunk across all 6 questions). Archaic Shakespearean vocabulary and dense dialogue cause keyword matching to return irrelevant character exchanges.

3. **Hit Rate $\neq$ Evidence Completeness**:
   Both Semantic and RRF Hybrid search achieved **100% Hit Rate@10** (retrieving $\ge 1$ gold evidence chunk for all 6 questions). However, **Evidence Completeness is only 33.3% for RRF** (and 50.0% for Semantic), demonstrating that retrieving isolated hit chunks does not equal retrieving the complete evidence set.

4. **Redundancy Consumes Top-K Capacity**:
   **48.3% of RRF Top-10 retrieved chunks** (and **56.7% of Semantic chunks**) represent redundant section duplicates from identical parent elements, exhausting Top-10 slots.

