So where are we now?

I would consider the overall investigation to have reached this point:

Phase 1 — Retrieval

We established that 50/50 RRF introduces BM25 noise and performs worse than semantic retrieval on the frozen benchmark.

Phase 2 — Evidence completeness

We established that low evidence completeness comes from a combination of:

ranking competition / BM25 noise
evidence distributed across chunks
retrieval-unit/context-boundary limitations

and that neighbor expansion can recover additional evidence, but at substantial context cost.

Phase 3 — End-to-end answer quality

We now have evidence that:

Recovering additional neighboring evidence does not automatically translate into better LLM answers.

For this benchmark, the Top-10 vector-dominant RRF already gave 94.4% answer completeness, and ±2 expansion did not improve it.